  "output": {
    "stories": [
      {
        "editor_spans": [
          {
            "body": "The spoken speech adds something the advance briefing left out, and it changes how the offer should be read. The one concrete thing on the table is not a set of new powers sent to councils around Edinburgh. It is 'DWP devolution': a model of localised employment support 'shaped by Holyrood'. The briefing, and my own earlier reading of it, made the case sound like an attack on Holyrood, with Westminster dealing directly with Scotland's regions. In fact the first real power offered would go to the Scottish Government. The talk of regions is the ambition. The actual transfer is from one parliament to the other, which is the kind of swap Burnham says devolution should go beyond.",
            "cites": [
              305,
              306,
              164,
              165,
              352,
              355
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "There is a catch in how the offer is made. Burnham's lesson from Easterhouse is that things work when they are 'run by the local community and the people on the estate.' Yet his offer comes with a condition set from the centre: Scotland's employment support 'has to be based on the Easterhouse principle. Has to be... If it's not based on that, it won't work.' What made one estate project work is now a requirement to be rolled out 'nationwide... the way we support everybody, everywhere.' Pilots that succeed because they are small, local and run by the people they serve often lose those qualities once they become national programmes with standard terms. Each time a minister falls for a local success, it risks becoming the next top-down scheme he says he wants to avoid.",
            "cites": [
              51,
              52,
              310,
              312,
              313,
              315,
              316,
              238
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The speech's own history also shows the limits of its remedy. Ravenscraig and the Leigh pits did not close because Motherwell or Leigh lacked control over employment support or local innovation funds. They closed because of decisions about state industries, energy and capital, made at national level and, for steel, driven by global markets. The new Scottish-UK defence colleges are worth £10 million from each government, and the research money is a billion pounds spread across the whole UK. Those sums show that the big levers are still held at the centre. Passing down the job of helping people get back into work is useful. But it gives places responsibility for dealing with the fallout, while the decisions that create a Ravenscraig stay at the top.",
            "cites": [
              82,
              84,
              92,
              96,
              175,
              213,
              299,
              305
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
